brighttpd is a small and fast static file server that i made to play with a few kernel toys like io_uring, kTLS, seccomp, and more.

idk where to put this but it beats nginx btw (read bench/benchmark.txt and bench/benchmark_tls.txt)

to reproduce this, run make bench and make bench-tls

you can use the Makefile at the root of this repository to build brighttpd. to do so, simply run make.

to run brighttpd, use brighttpd -c /path/to/brighttpd.conf (see more about the configuration format below)

for a dry-run: brighttpd -c configuration -n

the config format is like so (one directive per line, # comments):

  listen [::]:8080
  root /var/www/html

the config file must be 0600 and owned by the runner of the process.

the asset root must exist. if index.html or 404.html are missing, brighttpd uses fallback copies embedded into the binary.

workers install a seccomp-bpf syscall filter after startup setup is done. this is always enabled and has no config directive.

an example configuration is:

  # plain http bind. required for plain http mode
  # also required for tls mode if tls_redirect is yes
  listen [::]:8080

  # asset directory. index.html and 404.html may be provided here, otherwise
  # embedded fallback pages are used
  root /var/www

  # optional custom mime map. if omitted, a decent set of defaults is used
  mime_types /etc/nginx/mime.types

  # privilege drop after binding sockets.
  user www-data
  group www-data

  tls_listen [::]:8443
  tls_p12 /etc/ssl/private/site.p12
  tls_p12_passphrase password123
  tls_redirect yes

runtime behavior:

  byte ranges
    brighttpd supports single byte-range requests like Range: bytes=0-1023.
    malformed ranges and multipart ranges are ignored and served as normal
    200 responses. valid unsatisfiable ranges return 416.

  seccomp
    always enabled on worker threads after sockets, io_uring, pipes, and static
    files are set up. there is intentionally no directive to disable it.

all directives:

  listen
    the plain http bind address and port.
    example: listen [::]:8080
    required for plain http mode.
    also required for tls mode when tls_redirect is yes.

  tls_listen
    the https bind address and port.
    example: tls_listen [::]:8443
    only used when tls_p12 is set.

  root
    the asset root on disk.
    if index.html or 404.html are missing, embedded fallback pages linked into
    the binary are used.

  mime_types
    path to an nginx-style mime.types file.
    if omitted, brighttpd uses a set of defaults.

  tls_p12
    path to the pkcs#12 bundle for tls.
    enables tls mode.
    note: i don't support pem files because i'm just quirky. to convert your shitty pems to pkcs#12, run:
      openssl pkcs12 -export -out bundle.p12 -inkey private.key -in cert.pem -certfile chain.pem -passout pass:<password>

  tls_p12_passphrase
    passphrase for the pkcs#12 bundle.
    optional. omit it if the p12 is not encrypted.

  tls_redirect
    yes or no.
    default is yes.
    when yes, brighttpd starts a plain http listener on listen and returns redirects to the tls listener.
    when no, brighttpd only starts the tls listener.

  user
    user to drop to after sockets are bound.
    only works if the process started with enough privileges to drop user/group.

  group
    group to drop to after sockets are bound.

  workers
    number of worker threads, or auto.
    default: auto

  header_timeout
    seconds allowed to finish receiving request headers.
    default: 2

  tls_handshake_timeout
    seconds allowed to finish the tls handshake.
    default: 3

  keepalive_timeout
    idle keep-alive timeout in seconds.
    default: 5

  response_timeout
    max response lifetime in seconds before the connection gets closed.
    default: 30

  buffered_threshold
    do not touch this unless you're measuring things and you actually know why.
    files at or below this size are served from buffered reads instead of the splice path, in bytes.
    default: 8192

  ring_entries
    do not touch this unless you're measuring things and you actually know why.
    io_uring submission/completion queue size per worker.
    default: 1024

  request_buffer_size
    do not touch this unless you're measuring things and you actually know why.
    request header buffer size per connection, in bytes.
    default: 8192

  body_buffer_size
    do not touch this unless you're measuring things and you actually know why.
    buffered response chunk size per connection, in bytes.
    default: 16384

  max_connections
    do not touch this unless you're measuring things and you actually know why.
    max live connection slots per worker.
    default: 512

  listen_backlog
    do not touch this unless you're measuring things and you actually know why.
    listen backlog passed to listen(2).
    default: 8192

  submit_batch_threshold
    do not touch this unless you're measuring things and you actually know why.
    how many completions a worker handles before force-flushing queued uring
    submissions.
    default: 32

  splice_chunk_size
    do not touch this unless you're measuring things and you actually know why.
    max chunk size for splice streaming paths, in bytes.
    default: 1048576

  idle_scan_interval
    do not touch this unless you're measuring things and you actually know why.
    how often workers scan for timed-out connections, in seconds.
    default: 1

  tcp_defer_accept
    do not touch this unless you're measuring things and you actually know why.
    value passed to TCP_DEFER_ACCEPT on listeners.
    default: 1

  tcp_fastopen
    do not touch this unless you're measuring things and you actually know why.
    value passed to TCP_FASTOPEN on listeners.
    default: 4096
